  {
    "episode_id": 91090,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91090.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Well, the house is actually developing its own package. I think what the Senate did is insufficient for the urgency and the magnitude of the problem. So we're putting together a package that will, first of all, deal with working people, make certain that unemployment is extended to people who are in the gig economy, who are contract workers, make sure states are fully reimbursed for that unemployment. Rhode Island, as an example, went from having, you know, 10 people filed for unemployment on one day to 9,500 after the virus outbreak. So we need to be sure that states have the unemployment they need so folks can have the income that's necessary. We need to expand that coverage for all workers. We need to support small businesses, not just with loan programs, which we've already done, but with a grant program, because we have many small businesses. They've done the right thing. They've laid people off, sent them home. but they need money to keep the place open to pay their rent, pay their utilities, keep the operation open. And so we need to have a grant program to do that. And I expect that will be in our package. We need to have protections for mortgages and for folks so they don't lose their homes. We need to have a deferment on student loans. I'm advocating for a big investment in after-school funding and summer learning because we're going to have to make up for some of the educational losses for kids who have been sent home. We have to really importantly protect our healthcare workers. That was in the first proposal. It was taken out in the Senate by the Republicans, but we need to make sure we're protecting those that are on the front line of this pandemic. So we're going to have a very robust package that really deals with the economic implications of this for families and for working people. The focus of the Senate bill is on the big corporations. And while that's important to have airlines and other industries, with the impact on local tourism on restaurants and small businesses is significant. So we're going to have a very strong package. We have been working on it over the last week and I think it will respond both in scope and magnitude to the actual a challenge to be faces a state another",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
